<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#thewa">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thewa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thewa is a jewellery technique from Pratapgarh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in which finely worked sheet gold is fused onto coloured glass. The gold is engraved with openwork scenes of hunting, mythology or court life, and the glass beneath is usually green, red or blue. The craft has long been kept within a few families of goldsmiths of Pratapgarh, who pass it down from one generation to the next.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name comes from a word of the local Rajasthani language meaning setting. The craft grew up in Pratapgarh district, and its origin is placed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> era. The glass is treated to give a glittering effect that sets off the gold work. Each piece is made by hand and can take up to a month. The designs, cut freehand, draw on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> mythology, Mughal court scenes, historical events and plants and animals. Pratapgarh also makes Thewa on coloured glass bases embossed with gold miniature work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Descriptions of the process differ. In one, broken terracotta is ground fine and mixed with chemicals and oil into a thick paste spread on a wooden base; a sheet of 23-carat gold of 40 gauge is set on it and the design etched freehand. Black paint spread over the sheet makes the design stand out for finer work with small tools, and the excess gold is removed. In another, the gold is beaten into a very thin sheet, the Thewa Ki Patti, and inscribed with a fine chisel. It is pressed onto a lac-resin compound warmed on a board, the open pattern is knocked out, and the sheet is peeled away with heat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The process is credited to Nathu ji Soni in 1767. Its secrets have passed from father to son over the generations, among goldsmiths who call themselves the Raj-Sonis. Fine examples are held in museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and abroad, including the Metropolitan Museum of Art and the Victoria &amp; Albert. Ramprasad Rajsoni was the first to be honoured for the craft, in 1966, and Jagdish Lal Raj Soni, Beni Ram Soni and Girish Rajsoni were among those given the Shilp Guru Award. Mahesh Raj Soni received the Padma Shree in 2015, and members of the family have received the UNESCO Seal of Excellence Award.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thewa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Local goldsmiths developed <b>Thewa</b>, a technique of fusing gold filigree onto coloured glass.</blockquote><a href="/state/partabgarh-state">Partabgarh State</a></li></ul></section>
